import gunbc.host_names.gen_z { host_name_first_parts, gen_z_last_parts }

// A HUMAN NAME HAS THREE SHAPES AND NO OTHER: a middle part exists only beside a last part, so
// that invalid state has no constructor rather than a validator. Each part is one DNS label, and the
// rendered name joins them with "-", which keeps the rendering itself a DNS label.
type HumanName
  = FirstOnly { first: DnsLabel }
  | FirstLast { first: DnsLabel, last: DnsLabel }
  | FirstMiddleLast { first: DnsLabel, middle: DnsLabel, last: DnsLabel }

// THE DRAW IS RECORDED, AND THE NAME IT YIELDED IS RECORDED BESIDE IT. A name re-derived from its
// entropy on every read would silently rename every drawn host the day the corpus or the collision
// set changed, and A3 grounds agreement only on what stays stable across time. So the entropy is
// the receipt of how the name was chosen, the name is the allocated fact, and
// test.claim.hostname_allocation_witness re-runs the real allocator over each recorded draw so a
// recorded name the allocator would not have produced reds rather than standing.
type HumanNameDraw {
  entropy: Int
}

type AllocatedHostName
  = LegacyHostname { label: DnsLabel }
  | DrawnHumanName { name: HumanName, draw: HumanNameDraw }

// THE FLEET MACHINE REGISTRY ROW (gunbc.plans.fleet_machine_authority 2.1). `fleet_slot` is the machine's
// stable handle; `name` is its human name, the one root every projected name (hostname, runner label,
// secret ids, DNS and tailnet names) derives from; `next_name` is an accepted draw not yet applied, so a
// rename is the transition that moves next_name into name and nothing else. Board bindings are a
// separate admission (gunbc.fleet.fleet_machine_unit_binding), keyed by the same handle.
type HostnameAllocation {
  fleet_slot: HostIdentity
  name: AllocatedHostName
  next_name: AllocatedHostName?
}

fn human_name_canonical_hostname(name: HumanName) -> DnsLabel {
  match name {
    FirstOnly { first: f } => f
    FirstLast { first: f, last: l } => join([f as String, "-", l as String], "") as DnsLabel
    FirstMiddleLast { first: f, middle: m, last: l } =>
      join([f as String, "-", m as String, "-", l as String], "") as DnsLabel
  }
}

fn allocated_canonical_hostname(allocation: HostnameAllocation) -> DnsLabel {
  match allocation.name {
    LegacyHostname { label: l } => l
    DrawnHumanName { name: n, draw: _ } => human_name_canonical_hostname(name: n)
  }
}

// Every spelling a host answers to as a SELECTOR: its stable identity, its allocated hostname, and
// for a drawn name each shorter form of that same name. The shorter forms are conveniences -- they
// resolve only when they pick out one host -- while the allocated hostname is the injective key.
fn human_name_short_forms(name: HumanName) -> List<String> {
  match name {
    FirstOnly { first: _ } => []
    FirstLast { first: f, last: _ } => [f as String]
    FirstMiddleLast { first: f, middle: _, last: l } =>
      [f as String, join([f as String, "-", l as String], "")]
  }
}

fn allocation_selector_forms(allocation: HostnameAllocation) -> List<String> {
  let exact = [allocation.fleet_slot as String, allocated_canonical_hostname(allocation: allocation) as String]
  match allocation.name {
    LegacyHostname { label: _ } => exact
    DrawnHumanName { name: n, draw: _ } => append(exact, human_name_short_forms(name: n))
  }
}

data gunbc_hostname_allocation_scope_disposition: Disposition = Terminal {
  reason: "Single authority for a fleet machine's human name, and the registry that holds it (gunbc.plans.fleet_machine_authority, operator rulings D1 and D12, 2026-11-10). A machine's HostIdentity is its stable handle; its human name is a separate, mutable, MODELED fact -- a drawn HumanName with its draw receipt, or a LegacyHostname -- and every name the machine answers to is derived from that human name at one site. What this module forbids is deriving a name by READING THE HANDLE'S SPELLING: that makes a hostname a coincidence of how a key was typed, so a handle that is not a legal label silently has no hostname and one that is silently claims a hostname nobody chose. The lookup therefore fails closed: an unregistered handle yields Absent, never a name reconstructed from it. srv1-srv4, mtcollins1 and the H11 carry LEGACY names that coincide with their handle's spelling; that coincidence is a stated row, not a derivation rule, and each is replaced by the transition that applies its next_name. ROLE INDEPENDENCE is structural: no function here takes a role, a ComputeHost or any placement fact, so a role change cannot reach a name."
}

data gunbc_hostname_allocation_injectivity_disposition: Disposition = Terminal {
  reason: "A hostname is a JOIN KEY that consumers copy — SSH known_hosts, DNS records, runner registrations, Docker contexts, Tailscale, sccache endpoints, certificate SANs — so two fleet slots claiming one canonical hostname is not a tidiness defect, it is two machines answering to one join key and every consumer resolving to whichever converged last. Both directions are refused: a slot allocated twice is an ambiguous desired state, a hostname claimed twice is the collision above. THE RUNG CLIMBED WHEN std.allocation_roster LANDED, and this row is rewritten in the same change rather than left asserting the state it used to describe. It previously reported MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE and named its next-rung trigger as a duplicate-refusing keyed roster carrier whose CONSTRUCTION cannot admit a repeated key — deferred then because std.occurrence_binding_candidates keys on ONE axis while this roster must stay injective on TWO. std.allocation_roster is that two-axis carrier: AllocationRoster is sole_constructor, its only producers are the empty roster and an allocate that returns AllocationPrimaryKeyRepeated or AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated rather than a roster when either axis is claimed, so NO ACCEPTED PROGRAM HOLDS a roster with a slot allocated twice or a hostname claimed twice. The verdict machinery this row used to cite is deleted, not wrapped: there is no consistency question left for a production lookup to consult, because an inconsistent roster is not a value. RUNG NOW: STRUCTURALLY GUARANTEED at the carrier — the invalid state is describable in source but no accepted program contains it. It is NOT structurally impossible, and saying so would be the inflation gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder names: the authored allocation list below is still an ordinary literal into which a person can write two colliding rows, and build_allocation_roster refuses it at ONE fallible ingress instead of every lookup guarding against it. ATTAINABLE CEILING is structural impossibility. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, stated as the capability and owned by std.allocation_roster rather than restated per consumer: a declaration form that builds a keyed carrier at authoring time, so rows never pass through an intermediate list in which a duplicate is expressible. THE PENDING AXIS (next_name, 2026-11-10) IS ADMITTED AT THE SAME INGRESS AND AT THE SAME RUNG: hostname_allocation_admission admits each machine's current and pending canonical names as claims through the same refusing carrier, so a pending name another machine answers to or awaits is AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated, and every production lookup (canonical_hostname_in_roster, next_name_for_fleet_slot) answers from an admitted roster or not at all. EXECUTED EVIDENCE for the rung claimed here: the duplicate-refusal witnesses in test.claim.hostname_allocation_witness remain enrolled and now assert std.allocation_roster's arms — a slot allocated twice, a hostname claimed twice, a pending name claimed twice, and a colliding roster serving nothing rather than a winner."
}

// THE ALGEBRA MOVED TO std.allocation_roster, AND THAT MOVE FIRED THIS MODULE OWN DECLARED TRIGGER.
// The injectivity disposition above reported this roster as MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE and named its
// next-rung trigger as a duplicate-refusing keyed roster carrier whose CONSTRUCTION cannot admit a
// repeated key -- deferred at the time because std.occurrence_binding_candidates keys on one axis
// and this roster must stay injective on two. std.allocation_roster is that two-axis carrier, and it
// is sole_constructor with refusing producers, so no accepted program holds a roster with a slot
// allocated twice or a hostname claimed twice.
// WHAT THE VERDICT MACHINERY HERE BECAME: deleted, not wrapped. It existed to let the production
// lookup ask whether the roster was consistent before answering, and a roster value can no longer be
// inconsistent, so the question has no subject. The DESIGN dissolution rule -- a climb deletes the
// redundant lower-rung PRODUCTION machinery it obsoletes, while the discriminating REDs stay
// enrolled -- is why the scan record and the three-arm verdict are gone from this module while the
// duplicate-refusal tests remain, now asserting the shared carrier arms.
// THE RESIDUAL IS UNCHANGED IN KIND AND SMALLER IN SURFACE: the authored list below is still an
// ordinary literal a person can write two colliding rows into, and the fold refuses it at one
// ingress instead of every lookup guarding against it. That residual and its own next-rung trigger
// are stated once, in std.allocation_roster.
fn canonical_hostname_in_roster(
  allocations: List<HostnameAllocation>,
  fleet_slot: HostIdentity,
) -> DnsLabel? {
  match hostname_allocation_admission(allocations: allocations) {
    AllocationAdmitted { roster: roster } => allocation_lookup(roster: roster, wanted: fleet_slot as String)
    AllocationPrimaryKeyRepeated { key: _ } => Absent
    AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated { key: _ } => Absent
  }
}

// A NAME A MACHINE ANSWERS TO OR AWAITS. The registry's exclusive name claims are each machine's current
// canonical hostname and its pending one (gunbc.plans.fleet_machine_authority 2.5): no two claims may share a
// spelling, whichever machine and whichever axis they come from. Short forms of a drawn name are not claims;
// they are selector forms, and two machines sharing one is the resolver's HostSelectorAmbiguous, not a
// collision.
type HostNameClaim {
  machine: HostIdentity
  spelling: String
}

fn allocation_name_claims(allocation: HostnameAllocation) -> List<HostNameClaim> {
  concat(
    [HostNameClaim { machine: allocation.fleet_slot, spelling: allocated_canonical_hostname(allocation: allocation) as String }],
    map(allocation_pending_forms(allocation: allocation), f => HostNameClaim { machine: allocation.fleet_slot, spelling: f }),
  )
}

// THE ONE INGRESS, ON BOTH AXES. The handle/current-name roster is built first (a handle twice is
// AllocationPrimaryKeyRepeated, a current name twice AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated); the name claims are then
// admitted through the same refusing carrier keyed on the spelling, so a pending name another machine answers
// to or also awaits refuses as AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated naming it. Every production lookup here goes
// through this admission, so a colliding registry serves no hostname rather than a winner. A next_name equal to
// its own machine's current name is also refused: it names no rename.
fn hostname_allocation_admission(
  allocations: List<HostnameAllocation>,
) -> AllocationAdmission<DnsLabel> {
  match build_allocation_roster(
    rows: allocations,
    primary: row => row.fleet_slot as String,
    secondary: row => allocated_canonical_hostname(allocation: row) as String,
    value: row => allocated_canonical_hostname(allocation: row),
  ) {
    AllocationPrimaryKeyRepeated { key: k } => AllocationPrimaryKeyRepeated { key: k }
    AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated { key: k } => AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated { key: k }
    AllocationAdmitted { roster: roster } =>
      match build_allocation_roster(
        rows: flat_map(allocations, a => allocation_name_claims(allocation: a)),
        primary: c => c.spelling,
        secondary: c => c.spelling,
        value: c => c.machine,
      ) {
        AllocationAdmitted { roster: _ } => AllocationAdmitted { roster: roster }
        AllocationPrimaryKeyRepeated { key: k } => AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated { key: k }
        AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated { key: k } => AllocationSecondaryKeyRepeated { key: k }
      }
  }
}

// THE FIFTH SLOT, AND IT IS ALLOCATED RATHER THAN INHERITED. This module's scope disposition states
// that srv1-srv4's hostname-equals-slot-spelling is a legacy COINCIDENCE recorded as stated rows,
// never a derivation rule, and that a fifth slot must be allocated a hostname rather than inherit
// one. So this row is an authored allocation that happens to coincide with the identity's spelling,
// which is a different fact from being derived from it.
//
// WHY IT WAS NOT AUTHORED BY THE LANE THAT NEEDED IT. gunbc.host.host_reset_return_run compares an
// observer's own hostname against the subject's allocated one, and with no row here that check
// refused as unreadable. Guessing a hostname to clear that refusal would have been a fabricated
// observation standing exactly where the check that catches fabrication runs -- the guess would
// have disarmed the instrument it was feeding. The allocation is therefore a decision taken here
// and consumed there, which is the only ordering in which the check keeps its meaning.
//
// THE TWO DRAWN ROWS (2026-10-07) are the Hetzner Cloud CAX41 guests. Their stable identities are
// opaque (gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_identity), so nothing about provider, architecture or role is read off a
// spelling; their names were DRAWN by draw_human_name over the Gen Z naming scheme (gunbc.host_names.gen_z), with the entropy
// recorded as the receipt of the draw.
//
// A THIRD DRAWN ROW, "owen" (entropy 709187867323, drawn 2026-10-16 for host-94c81d), was deleted on
// 2026-11-05: host-94c81d named the same board as srv14, which already holds "butch", so the board had two
// hostnames (gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_identity, the ONE BOARD, ONE SLOT note). The draw is in git before
